## Experimental section
## Materials and preparation
Materials: (1S,2S)- $(+)$ -Pseudoephedrine, (1R,2R)- $(-)$ -Pseudoephedrine were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. 4-carboxybenzenediazonium tosylate (ADT-COOH) was prepared according to a published procedure (Y. Kalachyova et al., 2017). The ligand N-(4-pyridylmethyl)-l-alanine⋅HCl (l-HL) was synthesized according to (Pan et al., 2015). Au gratings were prepared according to (Guselnikova et al., 2020). Human serum (from human male AB plasma, USA origin, sterile-filtered, Sigma product number H4522) and human plasma (Citrated plasma, Sigma product number P9523) were used.
Preparation of Au mesoporous films: Au mesoporous films were prepared according to our previous report (Li et al., 2015). Briefly, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of pol­ ystyrene- $\cdot b$ -poly(ethylene oxide) (PS-b-PEO) was dissolved in $3\mathrm{mL}$ of THF completely at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , followed by the addition of $1.5\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol to the solution. An aqueous solution of $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ (the final concentration was $5\mathrm{mM}$ in $\mathrm{8~mL}$ of electrolyte) was added slowly to the clear PS-b-PEO solution, and spherical micelles were formed in the presence of water and THF, which are hydrophilic and hydrophobic, respectively. Electrochemical deposition from the precursor solutions was performed using electrochemical workstations (CHI 660E and 760E, CH Instruments, USA) with a conventional three-electrode system, including a platinum wire as the counter electrode, $\mathtt{A g/A g C l}$ as the reference electrode, and a conducting substrate as the working electrode. The electrodeposition of Au was performed at a constant potential of $-0.5\mathrm{V}$ (versus Ag/AgCl reference potential) for 1000 s without stirring at room temperature. After Au deposition, the micelles (used as soft-templates) were thoroughly washed with THF solution at $40\ {}^{\circ}{\bf C},$ , followed by drying with $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ flow.
Diazonium modification: Mesoporous Au films were spontaneously modified by soaking in a freshly prepared $3\mathrm{mM}$ aqueous solution of ADT-COOH for $40\mathrm{min}$ . After modification, the Au films were rinsed under sonication sequentially with water, methanol, and acetone for $10\mathrm{min}$ and finally dried in air.
Preparation of L/D-AlaZnCl: $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ (0.022 g, 0.1 mmol) and 1-HL $(0.048~\mathrm{g},0.2\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of EtOH. The solution was then transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave, followed by heating at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The reaction products were cooled to room temperature, and the solids were collected by centrifugation, washed with MeOH, and dried at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{h}}$ . After centrifugation, the mother liquid was further used for VAC growth of AlaZnCl films.
Surface-assisted growth of L/D-AlaZnCl: For film formation using VAC, a glass bottle $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{\tiny~100mL}}$ ) with a PBT cap equipped with a Teflon seal was used. The bottom part of the bottle was filled with Raschig-rings $(10\times10\mathrm{mm}^{2})$ to obtain an elevated flat platform for the substrate. Then, EtOH was added to the bottle. Afterwards, a substrate $(0.3\times1\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ was placed on the top of the Raschig-rings and fully coated with a drop of fresh MOF mother liquid. The bottle was closed and transferred to a preheated oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . Then, the bottle was removed from the oven and allowed to cool for $10\mathrm{min}$ before the substrate was removed, washed with EtOH, and dried. See the experimental setup in Fig. S2.
Measurement techniques
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed using an Omicron Nanotechnology ESCAProbeP spectrometer with a monochromated Al K Alpha X-ray source operating at $1486.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The spectra were recorded using an Omicron Nanotechnology ESCAProbeP spectrometer fitted with a monochromated Al K Alpha X-ray source. The pass energy was set at X (eV) and Y as intensity (cps) for the survey and high-resolution spectra. The energy resolution was $0.4\mathrm{eV}$ for the survey study and $0.1\mathrm{eV}$ for the high-resolution investigation. The analyzed area was $200\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ . The concentrations of elements were calculated in at. $\%$ using the sensitivity factors provided by the manufacturer.
SEM images were obtained using a LYRA3 GMU, Tescan, CR setup with a $10\mathrm{kV}$ accelerating voltage. Before analysis, the samples were coated with $10{\cdot}\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{Pt}$ layer. The surface morphology was characterized using the peak-force AFM technique (Icon, Brücker). AFM scratch tests were performed on Au films, deposited on microscopic glass substrates, by profiling across a scratch, where the values are reported as the means of 20 values obtained for ten scratches on five samples. XRD data were obtained using an XRD-diffractometer PANalytical X’Pert PRO using $\operatorname{Cu}\mathrm{K}\upalpha$ radiation $(1.5406\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ .
Cyclic Voltammetry (CVA) measurements were performed in a solution of $5~\mathrm{mM}$ potassium hexacyanoferrate (II) trihydrate in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ potassium chloride. ECSA measurements were performed in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\cal O}_{4}$ . CVA measurements were performed using a Palm Sens 4 device (Palm Instruments, Netherlands) controlled by PSTrace 5.3 software (March 5, 1127 firmware). The samples were used as the working electrode, a Pt wire was used as the counter electrode, and measurements were performed with a $\mathtt{A g/A g C l}$ (3 M KCl) reference electrode.
Raman spectroscopy measurements
SERS spectra were measured using a ProRaman-L Raman spectrometer (with a laser power of $70\mathrm{mW}$ and an excitation wavelength of $785\mathrm{nm}$ ), and a Thermo Scientific DXR Raman Microscope (with a laser power $30\mathrm{mW}$ and an excitation wavelength of $632\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ). The spectra were measured 100 times with a 1 s accumulation time for each measurement. The Raman spectra were used for chemical mapping with a surface area of $1.0\times1.0\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ with $50\times50$ points spaced by a gap of $0.02~\mathrm{{mm}}$ . The same Raman spectrometer ( $785~\mathrm{nm}$ and $70~\mathrm{{mW}}$ ) was used for mapping. The spectra were recorded at a resolution of $2\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the $2000{-}500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ wavenumber range with an exposure time of $100s$ for each exposure. To calculate the RSD from the SERS map, Gwyddion software (Statistical Quantities Tool) was used. For the calculation of RSD between samples, five samples of Au-LAlaZnCl were tested for sensing $10^{-8}$ M $(+)$ -PE and $10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~VN}$ in undiluted citrated human blood plasma. Ten spectra were collected from each sample and the average values were used for the RSD calculation.
Calculation of LOD and SNR: The LOD was calculated using the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), according to the IUPAC recommendations (INTER­ NATIONAL, n.d). In particular, the minimal concentration at which the signal-to-noise relation is equal to 3 was defined as the LOD. The standard deviation (SD) of the background signal (noise) was calculated from 20 spectra according to relation $S D=\sqrt{\sum{|\frac{x-\overline{{x}}}{N}|}}$ , where $\mathbf{x}$ is the intensity of the Raman signal at $972~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $\textstyle{\overline{{x}}}$ is the mean of the intensities, and $N$ is the number of spectra used. Accordingly, we calculated an $\boldsymbol{s D}$ of 3.2. The SNR was calculated using:
$$
S N R=\ \frac{\overline{{S}}}{S D}
$$
where $\overline{s}$ is the mean average peak height above the background. This SNR was calculated from 20 samples $\left(\Nu=20\right)$ ) of $(+)\mathrm{PE}+(\mathrm{BSA})$ . Gas chromatography measurements
An 8860 Agilent gas chromatograph equipped with flame ionization detector and fitted with an 8200 Autosampler was used for the crossvalidation of the SERS results. The analytes were separated using an HP-5 column $(30~\mathrm{m}\times0.25~\mathrm{mm}\times0.25~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . Helium was used as the carrier gas at a flow rate of $0.7\mathrm{mL/min}$ . The injection was performed in splitless mode with a volume of $10.0\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . The injector and interface temperatures were both maintained at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The initial column temperature was set at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and held for $2\mathrm{min}$ , followed by temperature ramps of $15\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ to $230^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and held for $2\:\mathrm{min}$ . The total run time was $12.50\mathrm{min}$ .
Sample preparation for GC
A stock solution of PE with a concentration of $10^{-2}\mathrm{M}$ was prepared by dissolving $165\mathrm{mg}$ of PE in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ ethyl acetate. To prepare a calibration curve, aqueous solutions of ephedrine at concentrations of $10^{-4}$ , $10^{-5}$ , $10^{-6}$ , ${{10}^{-7}}$ , $10^{-8}$ , $10^{-9}$ , $10^{-10}$ , $10^{-11}$ , $10^{-\dot{1}2},10^{-13}$ , and $10^{-14}\mathrm{M}$ were mixed with $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of saturated sodium bicarbonate, pH was adjusted to 9.0 with $1.0\mathrm{M}$ sodium carbonate and ethyl acetate. The tubes were vortexed for 30 min and centrifuged $(3000~\mathrm{rpm})$ for $3\mathrm{min}$ . The ethyl acetate was evaporated to $1~\mathrm{mL}$ and analyzed by GC.
$0.48~\mathrm{mL}$ of blood plasma $+10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ vancomycin solution $+10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of PE solution with concentrations $10^{-8}$ , $10^{-12}$ , $10^{-14}\mathrm{M}$ , and $20{\upmu\mathrm{L}}=$ resulting solution were deposited to Au-L-AlaZnCl samples for $30~\mathrm{min}$ at $3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , washed with water, dried, and analyzed by SERS. To the residual solution, $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of saturated sodium bicarbonate was added to adjust the $\tt p H$ to 9.0, with $1.0\mathrm{M}$ sodium carbonate and $4.0\mathrm{mL}$ of ethyl acetate in tubes was added. The tubes were vortexed for $30\mathrm{min}$ and centrifuged ( $3000\mathrm{rpm})$ for $3\mathrm{min}$ . The ethyl acetate was evaporated to $1\mathrm{mL}$ and analyzed by GC.
